\id SNG Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Canticle of Canticles
\toc1 Salomon's Canticle of Canticles which in Hebrew is called Sir Hasirim. שיר השירים
\toc2 Canticle of Canticles
\toc3 SNG
\mt1 Salomon's Canticle of Canticles which in Hebrew is called Sir Hasirim. שיר השירים
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE CANTICLE OF CANTICLES.
\ip \it SALOMON\it*, called alſo \it Eccleſiaſtes\it*, and \it Idida\it*, according to theſe three names (as S. Ierom \f - \ft King Salomon according to his three names writte and intitled his three books.\f* noteth) writte three bookes of three particular arguments, directed to three degrees of people, \f - \ft \+it Proem. in Eccle.\+it*\f* with three diſtinct titles, al tending to one end, the true ſeruice of God, which bringeth to eternal felicitie. In the firſt he teacheth the principles of good life, to fly from vices, and folow vertues:
\ip belonging to ſuch as \it beginne\it* to obſerue Gods law, wherin true wiſdom conſiſteth: and this booke is called the \it Prouerbes\it*, or \it Parables\it*, that is to ſay, Pithie, brief, ſentencious precepts, of \it Salomon\it*, which ſignifieth \it Pacificus, Peaceable\it*, or Pacifier: \it the ſonne of Dauid, King of Iſrael\it*. In the ſecond he \f - \ft Salomon Pacifier king of Iſrael.\f* exhorteth to contemne this world, shewing that true felicitie conſiſteth not in anie worldlie or temporal thinges, but in the eternal fruition of God, which is obtayned by keping his commandments. And this booke he intitleth: \it The wordes of Eccleſiaſtes\it*, which is Concionator, Preacher, \it Sonne of Dauid, King of Ieruſalem\it*, becauſe he there exhorteth ſuch as haue made ſome \f - \ft Eccleſiaſtes, Preacher king of Ieruſalem.\f* progreſſe in vertues, called \it Proficientes\it*, ſignified by the inhabitants of the Metropolitan citie Ieruſalem; whereas in the former he ſtiled himſelf King of Iſrael, propoſing precepts mete for al the twelue tribes, and al vulgar men deſirous and beginning to ſerue God: In both bookes, for more auctoritie ſake, making mention of his godlie renowmed father the Royal Prophet Dauid, with his owne title alſo of King. But in this third booke he only expreſſeth his proper name Salomon, whom God ſingularly loued, wherof he was called \it Idida\it*. Becauſe this alone, without mention of \f - \ft Idida, Beloued.\f* father or King, was moſt conuenient for the \it Perfect\it*, who not as ſeruants, or yong ſcholars are moued by feare of auctoritie, but as children are ſwetly drawne by loue. And this he writte in verſe, intitling it not ſimply a Canticle, but \it The Canticle of Canticles\it*, as preeminent aboue other \f - \ft This canticle doth excel other Canticles.\f* Canticles: The bridal ſongue for the Mariage, to be ſolemnized betwen God himſelf and his glorious ſpouſe. For though al holie Scriptures are the ſpiritual bread, and food of the faithful, yet al \f - \ft Al are not mete to read it.\f* are not meate for al, at al ſeaſons. Some parts are not for ſinners, nor for beginners, nor for ſuch as are yet in the way towards perfection, but only for the perfect. According to the Apoſtles doctrine: \it Milke is for children, that are yet vnskilful of the word of iuſtice. But ſtrong meate is for the perfect,\it* \f - \ft \+it Heb. 5.\+it*\f* \it them that by cuſtom, haue their ſenſes exerciſed to the diſcerning of good and euil\it*. With what moderation therfore and humilitie, this Canticle of Gods perfect ſpouſe may be read, the diſcrete wil conſider, and not preſume aboue their reach, but be wiſe with ſobrietie. For here be very high and hidden Myſteries, as Origen teacheth in his lerned Commentaries (which Saint Ierom tranſlated into Latin and ſingularly commendeth) and ſo much harder to be rightly vnderſtood, for that the feruent ſpiritual loue, of the inward man, reformed in ſoule, and perfected in ſpirite, is here Vttered in the ſame vſual wordes and termes, wherwith natural, worldlie, yea and carnal loue of the outward man, old Adam, corrupted by ſinne, is commonly expreſſed: and are ſo much more dangerous to be miſtaken, as we are more addicted to proper wil, & priuate iudgement, or ſubiect to carnal, or paſſionate motions. Wherfore it ſemeth moſt mete to kepe the ſame order in \f - \ft Beſt methode in lerning is to beginne with doctrine of good life, then ſtudie to know natural thinges: and finally contemplate diuine myſteries.\f* reading theſe three bookes, which the auctor wiſe Salomon obſerued in writing them. And which Philoſophers alſo folow in their forme of diſcipline. For they firſt learne and teach Moral Philoſophie, then Natural & laſtly Metaphiſikes, which is their Diuinitie. As Salomon had given them example: firſt teaching \it precepts of good\it* life and maners, \it in\it* his \it Prouerbes\it*: after, diſcourſing of natural thinges \it in Eccleſiaſtes\it*, deduced thence a concluſion, which prophane Philoſophers wel vnderſtood not, \it to contemne this world\it*: and finally cometh to high myſtical Diuinitie, in this ſupereminent \it Canticle: written\it* in another ſtile, \it in verſe\it*, and \it in forme of a ſacred Dialogue\it* betwen Chriſt and his ſpouſe, or as Origen calleth it, in forme of an \it Enterlude\it*, in reſpect of \f - \ft A ſacred, dialogue or Enterlude.\f* \f - \ft \+it * Forma dramatis.\+it*\f* diuers ſpeakers & actors, & of diuers perſons, to whom the ſpeaches are directed, and of whom they are vttered. For \it by the Spous or Bridgrome\it*, is not only vnderſtood Chriſt as Man, but \f - \ft God & Chriſt the Spous, or Bridgrome.\f* alſo as God, and the whole Bleſſed Trinitie; to whom manie prayers, praiſes and thankes are offered vp; and by whom manie benefites are given, praiſes returned, & promiſes made to his ſpouſe. Likewiſe by the \it Spouſe\it* or \it Bride\it* the ancient fathers vnderſtand three ſortes of ſpouſes: al \f - \ft Three ſpouſes.\f* eſpouſed to Chriſt, and to God, to wit, his \it General Spouſe\it*, the whole Church of the old and \f - \ft The General.\f* new Teſtaments: of al that are, and shal be perfect, making one myſtical bodie, free from ſinne, without ſpotte or wrinkle, ſanctified in Chriſt. Alſo his ſpecial ſpouſe, which is euerie \f - \ft \+it Ephes. 5.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The ſpecial.\f* particular holie ſoule. And his ſingular ſpouſe, his moſt bleſſed & moſt immaculate Virgin \f - \ft and Singular.\f* Mother. This being the general ſumme of this excellent Canticle, remitting the reader, for explication therof to the lerned deuout Commenters, both of ancient and late writers, we shal alſo \f - \ft \+it Origen. S. Ierom. S. Aug. lib. 8. de Gen. ad li. S. Greg. S. Beda. S. Tho. Arbor. Geneb. Del Rio.\+it*\f* endeuour to gather the ſame contents more particularly, not before the chapters, becauſe we cannot there ſo conueniently diſtinguish the ſame by verſes, but in the margent: where we \f - \ft The particular contents are ſette in the margent of euerie chapter.\f* shal eſpecially note the ſpeakers, as ſemeth more probable, of euerie parcel, according to the firſt ſenſe (not hauing room for more) perteyning to the General ſpouſe, the Catholique Church: which is the great, and euerlaſting holie Citie of God the eternal King.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 LET \f a \fr 1.1 \ft The Church of the old teſtament deſireth Chriſts coming in flesh: and the Chriſtian Church prayeth for his coming in glorie.\f* him kiſſe me with the kiſſe of his mouth: becauſe thy breſtes are better then wine,
\v 2 ſmelling fragrantly of the beſt ointments. Oile powred out is thy name: therfore haue yongmaydes loued thee.
\v 3 Draw me: we wil runne after thee in the odour of thine ointments. The king hath brought me into his cellars: we wil reioyce & be glad in thee, mindful of thy breſts aboue wine: the righteous loue thee.
\v 4 \f b \fr 1.4 \ft The Church outwardly afflicted, is inwardly fayre.\f* I am blacke but beutiful, ô ye daughters of Ieruſalem, as the tabernacles of Cedar, as the skinnes of Salomon.
\v 5 Doe not conſider me that I am browne, becauſe the ſunne hath altered my colour: the ſonnes of my mother haue fought againſt me, they haue made me a keeper in the vinyards: my vinyard I haue not kept.
\v 6 Shew me ô thou, whom my ſoule loueth, where thou feedeſt, where thou lyeſt in the midday, leſt I beginne to wander after the flockes of thy companyons.
\v 7 \f c \fr 1.7 \ft Chriſt encorageth his ſpouſe the Church.\f* If thou know not thyſelfe, ô moſt fayreſt among wemen, goeforth, and folow after the ſteppes of the flockes, and feede thy kiddes byſide the tabernacles of the paſtours.
\v 8 To my companie of horſemen, in the chariotes of Pharao, haue I likened thee, ô my loue.
\v 9 Thy cheekes are beautiful as the turteldoues, thy necke as iewels.
\v 10 We wil make thee cheynes of gold, enamoled with ſiluer.
\v 11 \f d \fr 1.11 \ft She meditateth of his Paſſion, and Reſurrection.\f* Whiles the king was at his repoſe, my ſpikenard gaue the odour thereof.
\v 12 A bundle of myrrhe my beloued is to me, he shal abide betwen my breſtes.
\v 13 A cluſtre of cypre my loue is to me, in the vineyardes of Engaddi.
\v 14 \f e \fr 1.14 \ft Chriſt praiſeth his ſpouſe\f* Behold thou art fayre, ô my loue, behold thou art fayre, thyne eyes are as of doues.
\v 15 \f f \fr 1.15 \ft She againe praiſeth him;\f* Behold thou art fayre my beloued, & comlie: \f g \fr 1.15 \ft With thankes for her repoſe, and preſent conſolation.\f* our litle bed is florishing.
\v 16 The beames of our houſes are of cedar, our rafters of cypreſſe trees.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 I AM \f a \fr 2.1 \ft Chriſt profeſſeth himſelf the floure of mankinde: yea Lord of al creatures.\f* the flower of the filde, and the lilie of the valley.
\v 2 \f b \fr 2.2 \ft The Church excelleth al other ſocieties: In the Church the godlie excel ſinners, Among the innocent and holie, the virgin Marie ſurpaſſeth al.\f* As the lilie among the thornes, ſo is my loue among the daughters.
\v 3 \f c \fr 2.3 \ft The Church praiſing Chriſt reſteth ſecure vnder his protectiõ.\f* As the apletree among trees of the woddes, ſo is my beloued among the ſonnes. Vnder his shadow, whom I deſired, I ſate: and his fruite was ſweete vnto my throte.
\v 4 He brought me into the winceller, he hath ordered in me charitie.
\v 5 Stay me vp with flowers, compaſſe me about with apples: becauſe I languish with loue.
\v 6 His lefthand vnder my head, and his righthand shal embrace me.
\v 7 I adiure you ô daughters of Ieruſalem, by the roes, and the hartes of the fildes, \f d \fr 2.7 \ft He for the weakes ſake permitteth her not to be moleſted, til she be prepared to ſuffer with patience.\f* that you rayſe not, nor make the beloued to awake, vntil herſelfe wil.
\v 8 \f e \fr 2.8 \ft She feeling Chriſts aſſiſtance, confeſſeth, & preacheth boldly his Goſpel, & truth againſt al Paganes, and Heretikes.\f* The voice of my beloued, behold he cometh leaping in the mountaines, leaping ouer the little hilles:
\v 9 my beloued is like vnto a roe, and to a fawne of hartes. Behold he ſtandeth behind our walle, \f f \fr 2.9 \ft Who though he ſhew not himſelf viſibly:\f* looking through the windowes, looking forth by the grates.
\v 10 Behold my beloued ſpeaketh to me: \f g \fr 2.10 \ft yet encorageth her to approch vnto him:\f* Ariſe, make haſt my loue, my doue, beautiful one, and come.
\v 11 For winter is now paſt, the rayne is gone, and departed.
\v 12 The flowers haue appeared in our land, the time of pruning is come: the voice of the turtledoue is heard in our land:
\v 13 the figgetree hath brought forth her greene figges: the florishing vineyards haue geuen their ſauour. Ariſe my loue, my beautiful one, & come.
\v 14 My doue in the holes of the rocke, in the holow places of the wal, shew me thy face, let thy voice ſound in mine eares: for thy voice is ſweete, and thy face comely.
\v 15 \f h \fr 2.15 \ft commandeth his paſtors to deſtroy hereſies.\f* Catch vs the litle foxes, that deſtroy the vineyards: for our vineyard hath florished.
\v 16 \f i \fr 2.16 \ft And ſo she repoſeth in him.\f* My beloued to me, and I to him, who feedeth among the lilies,
\v 17 til the day breake, and the shadowes decline. Returne: be like, my beloued, to a roe, and to the fawne of hartes vpon the mountaynes of Bether.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 IN \f a \fr 3.1 \ft The Church finding Chriſt not in darke ignorance nor in philoſophie but by his reueiling himſelfe to her, holdeth him for euer:\f* my litle bed in the nightes I haue ſought him, whom my ſoule loueth, I haue ſought him, and haue not found.
\v 2 I wil riſe, and wil goe about the citie: by the ſtreates and high waies, I wil ſeeke him whom my ſoule loueth: I haue ſought him, and haue not found.
\v 3 The watchmen which kepe the citie found me; Haue you ſeene him, whom my ſoule loueth.
\v 4 When I had a litle paſſed by them, I found him whom my ſoule loueth: I held him: neither wil I let him goe, til I bring him into \f b \fr 3.4 \ft euen til the Iewes shal at laſt alſo find him.\f* my mothers houſe, and into the chamber of her that bare me.
\v 5 \f c \fr 3.5 \ft Chriſt ſpeaketh as before ch. 2. v. 7.\f* I adiure you ô daughters of Ieruſalem by the roes, and the hartes of the fildes, that you rayſe not vp, nor make the beloued to awake, til herſelfe wil.
\v 6 \f d \fr 3.6 \ft The Church of Chriſt admireth her owne conuerſion from Gentilitie,\f* What is she, that aſcendeth by the deſert, as a litle rod of ſmoke of \f e \fr 3.6 \ft now ful of good workes.\f* the aromatical ſpices of myrtrhe, and frankincenſe, & of al powder of the apothecharie?
\v 7 \f f \fr 3.7 \ft She alſo profeſſeth that the aſcending to eternal reſt, is by fighting manfully, in obſeruing the ten commandments, in the ſix dayes of this life:\f* Behold threeſcore valiants of the moſt valiant of Iſrael, compaſſe the litle bed of Salomon:
\v 8 al holding ſwordes, and moſt cunning to battels: euerie mans ſword vpon his thigh for feares by night.
\v 9 King Salomon hath made him a portable throne of the wood of Libanus:
\v 10 the pillers therof he hath made of ſiluer, the ſeate of gold, the going vp \f g \fr 3.10 \ft euen to bloud, if nede be,\f* of purple: the middes he hath paued with \f h \fr 3.10 \ft which is the higheſt degree of charitie.\f* charitie for the daughters of Ieruſalem.
\v 11 \f i \fr 3.11 \ft And inuiteth al others to come vnto Chriſt,\f* Goe forth ye daughters of Sion, and ſee king Salomon in \f k \fr 3.11 \ft who in the flesh which he tooke of his mother, was crowned in heauen after his Paſſion.\f* the diademe, wherewith his mother hath crowned him in the day of his deſpouſing, and in the day of the ioy of his heart.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 HOW \f a \fr 4.1 \ft Chriſt againe praieth the beautie of his Church.\f* beautiful art thou my loue, how beautiful art thou! thine \f b \fr 4.1 \ft Sincere and ſimple intention.\f* eies as it were of doues, beſides that, which lyeth hid within. Thy \f c \fr 4.1 \ft Al her temporal occupations directed to Gods glorie\f* heares as the flockes of goates, which haue come vp from mount Galaad.
\v 2 Thy \f d \fr 4.2 \ft Paſtors who like nurces geue bread of good doctrine to litle ones.\f* teeth as flockes of them that are shorne, which haue come vp from the lauatorie, al with \f e \fr 4.2 \ft Faith and good workes.\f* twinnes, and there is no barren among them.
\v 3 Thy \f f \fr 4.3 \ft Preaching Chriſts paſſion.\f* lippes as a ſcarlet lace: and thy ſpeach ſweete. As à peece of a pomegranate, ſo are alſo thy \f g \fr 4.3 \ft And not ashamed to profeſſe Chriſt Crucified.\f* cheekes, beſides that which lyeth hid within.
\v 4 Thy \f h \fr 4.4 \ft Adminiſtration of Sacraments wherby the Church, Chriſts myſtical bodie, is ioyned to him her head,\f* necke is as the \f i \fr 4.4 \ft which is an inexpugnable fortreſſe.\f* towre of Dauid, which is built with bulworkes: a thouſand targattes hang on it, al the armour of the valiants.
\v 5 Thy \f k \fr 4.5 \ft Both Iewes and Gentiles are fed with the principles of Chriſtian doctrin.\f* two breaſtes as two fawnes the twinnes of a roe, which feede among the lilies,
\v 6 til the day aſpire, and the shadowes decline. \f l \fr 4.6 \ft Chriſt dwelleth in mortified, and deuout mindes.\f* I wil goe to the mount of myrrhe, and to the little hil of frankencenſe.
\v 7 Thou art al fayre ô my loue, and there is \f m \fr 4.7 \ft The Church triumphant is without ſpotte, and euerie particular ſoule entring into heauen; the B. virgin mother was alſo in this life alwayes immaculate.\f* not a ſpotte in thee.
\v 8 Come from Libanus my ſpouſe, come from Libanus, come: thou shalt be crowned from the head of Amena, from the toppe of Sanir & Hermon, from the dennes of lions, from the mountaynes of leopardes.
\v 9 Thou haſt wounded my heart, my ſiſter ſpouſe, thou haſt wounded my heart in one of thine eies, and in one heare of thy necke.
\v 10 How beautiful are thy breaſtes my ſiſter ſpouſe! thy breaſtes are more beautiful then wine, and the odour of thine ointmentes aboue al aromatical ſpices.
\v 11 Thy lippes my ſpouſe are as an honie combe diſtilling, honie and milke are vnder thy tongue: and the odour of thy garments as the odour of frankincenſe.
\v 12 My ſiſter ſpouſe is a garden incloſed, a garden incloſed, a fountaine ſealed vp.
\v 13 Thy offſprings a paradiſe of pomegranats with orchard fruites. Cypres with ſpiknard,
\v 14 ſpiknard, and ſafren, ſweete cane and cinnamon, with al the trees of Libanus, myrrhe and aloes with al the chiefe ointmentes.
\v 15 The fountaine of gardens: the wel of liuing waters, which runne with violence from Libanus.
\v 16 \f n \fr 4.16 \ft Al tentations, whether they be in manifeſt crueltie, or in flatering ſuteltie, make conſtant ſoules more gratful to God.\f* Ariſe Northwinde, & come Southwinde, blow through my garden, and let the aromatical ſpices therof flowe.
\c 5
\p
\v 1 LET \f a \fr 5.1 \ft The ſpouſe condeſcending to Gods wil, is wel content to ſuffer perſecution.\f* my beloued come into his garden, and eate the fruite of his appletrees. \f b \fr 5.1 \ft Chriſt againe sheweth his good liking in his ſpouſes patience:\f* I am come into my garden ô my ſiſter ſpouſe, I haue reaped my myrrhe, with myne aromatical ſpices: I haue eaten the honiecombe with mine honie, I haue drunke my wine with my milke: \f c \fr 5.1 \ft and willeth the glorious Sainctes to congratulate vvith the patient.\f* eate ô frendes, and drinke, and be inebriated my deareſt.
\v 2 \f d \fr 5.2 \ft The ſpouſe deſireth to reſt in meditation.\f* I ſleepe, and my hart watcheth: \f e \fr 5.2 \ft but is called vpon to helpe others:\f* the voice of my beloued knocking: Open to me my ſiſter, my loue, my doue, mine immaculate: \f f \fr 5.2 \ft and vrged by Chriſts owne example working for al mankind.\f* becauſe my head is ful of dew, and my lockes of the droppes of the nightes.
\v 3 I haue ſpoyled myſelfe of my robe, how shal I be clothed with it? I haue washed my feete, how shal I defile them?
\v 4 My beloued put his hand through the hôle, and my bellie trembled at his touch.
\v 5 \f g \fr 5.5 \ft And ſo she imployeth herſelf alſo in actiue life.\f* I aroſe, that I might open to my beloued: my handes haue diſtilled myrrhe, and my fingers are ful of moſt approued myrrhe.
\v 6 I opened the bolt of my dore to my beloued: but he had turned aſide, and was paſſed. My ſoule melted, as he ſpake: I ſought, and found him not: I called, and he did not anſwer me.
\v 7 The keepers that goe about the citie found me: they ſtroke me, and wounded me: the keepers of the walles tooke away my cloke.
\v 8 \f h \fr 5.8 \ft Stil conſeruing a deſire to returne vnto contemplation.\f* I adiure you ô daughters of Hieruſalem, if you shal finde my beloued, that you tel him, that I languishe with loue.
\v 9 \f i \fr 5.9 \ft The deuout confer together deſcribing the excellencies of Chriſt.\f* What maner of one is thy beloued of the beloued, ô moſt beautiful of wemen? What maner of one is thy beloued of the beloued, that thou haſt ſo adiured vs?
\v 10 My beloued is white and ruddie, choſen of thouſands.
\v 11 His head is as the beſt gold: his heares as the branches of palmetrees, blacke as à rauen.
\v 12 His eies as doues vpon the litle riuers of waters, which are washed with milke, and ſitte beſide the moſt ful ſtreames.
\v 13 His cheekes are as litle beddes of aromatical ſpices ſet of the pigmentaries. His lippes are as lilies diſtilling principal myrrhe.
\v 14 His handes wrought round of gold, ful of hyacinthes. His bellie of iuorie, diſtinguished with ſapphires.
\v 15 His thighes as pillers of marble, that are vpon feete of gold. His forme as of Libanus, elect as the cedars.
\v 16 His throte moſt ſweete, and he whole to be deſired: ſuch an one is my beloued, and he is my frend, ô daughters of Hieruſalem.
\v 17 \f k \fr 5.17 \ft And reſolue to ſeke him, whereſoeuer he be.\f* Whither is thy beloued gone ô moſt beautiful of wemen? whither is thy beloued turned aſide, and we wil ſeeke him with thee?
\c 6
\p
\v 1 MY \f a \fr 6.1 \ft The Church teacheth her children that Chriſt is delighted with the godlie deſires, and fructful workes of the faithful.\f* beloued is gone downe into his garden, to the bed of aromatical ſpices, to feede in the gardens, and to gather lilies.
\v 2 I to my beloued, and my beloued to me, who feedeth among the lilies.
\v 3 \f b \fr 6.3 \ft Chriſt againe commendeth his Church, wel compoſed of diſtinct orders (ſome gouerning, ſome retired in cloiſters from this world, the reſt alſo exerciſing workes of mercie, in the trubles of this life) al together making a complete armie, terrible to al enimies.\f* Thou art fayre ô my loue, ſweete, and comelie as Hieruſalem: terrible as the armie of a campe ſet in aray.
\v 4 \f c \fr 6.4 \ft The more anie contemplate Gods Maieſtie, the better they perceiue that he is incomprehenſible.\f* Turne away thine eies from me, becauſe they haue made me flee away. Thy heares as a flocke of goates, which haue appeared from Galaad.
\v 5 Thy teeth as à flocke of sheepe, which haue come vp from the lauatorie, al with twinnes, and there is no barren among them.
\v 6 As the barke of a pomegranate, ſo are thy cheekes beſide thy hidden.
\v 7 There are \f d \fr 6.7 \ft Manie true paſtores,\f* threeſcore queenes, & \f e \fr 6.7 \ft more hyrelinges, that alſo preach truth, but for temporal commoditie.\f* foureſcore concubines, and of \f f \fr 6.7 \ft And innumerable faithful ſoules in the Church.\f* yongmaydes there is no number.
\v 8 My doue is \f g \fr 6.8 \ft Al vvhich are but one bodie in vnitie of faith.\f* one, my perfect one, she is the only to her mother, elect to her that bare her. The daughters haue ſeene her, and declared her to be moſt bleſſed: the queenes and concubines, and haue prayſed her.
\v 9 \f h \fr 6.9 \ft The voice of the old ſynagogue, admiring the beautie of Chriſts Church.\f* What is she, that cometh forth as the morning ryſing, fayre as the moone, elect as the ſunne, terrible as the armie of a campe ſet in aray?
\v 10 I came downe into the garden of nuttes, to ſee the fruites of the valles, and to looke if the vineyarde had florished, and the pomegranats budded.
\v 11 I knew not: my ſoule trubled me for the chariotes of Aminadab.
\v 12 \f i \fr 6.12 \ft The Church of Chriſt exhorteth the Synagogue of the Ievves to returne to Chriſt.\f* Returne, returne ô Sulamiteſſe: returne, returne that we may behold thee.
\c 7
\p
\v 1 WHAT \f a \fr 7.1 \ft Chriſt interpoſeth his commendation of the Ievves, who at laſt shal returne to him with great feruoure of faith and deuotion.\f* shalt thou ſee in the Sulamiteſſe but the companies of campes? How beautiful are thy paſes in shoes, ô princes daughter! \f b \fr 7.1 \ft And ſo iointly praiſeth his Church conſiſting of both peoples.\f* the ioyntes of thy thighes are as iewels, that are made by the hand of the artificer.
\v 2 Thy nauel as à round bowle, neuer wanting cuppes. Thy bellie as an heape of wheate, compaſſed about with lilies.
\v 3 Thy two breſts, as two fawnes the twinnes of a roe.
\v 4 Thy necke as a towre of yuorie. Thine eies as the fishpooles in Heſebon, which are in the gate of the daughter of the multitude. Thy noſe as the towre of Libanus; that looketh againſt Damaſcus.
\v 5 Thy head as Carmelus: and the heares of thy head as a kings purple tyed to cundite pipes.
\v 6 How beautiful art thou, and how comely my deareſt, in delightes!
\v 7 Thy ſtature is like to a palmetree, & thy breaſtes to cluſters of grapes.
\v 8 I ſayd: I wil goe vp into the palmetree, and wil take hold of the fruites therof: and thy breaſts shal be as the cluſters of a vineyard: and the odour of thy mouth as it were of apples.
\v 9 Thy throate as the beſt wine, \f c \fr 7.9 \ft The Church, as it were taking the word out of Chriſts mouth vvhiles he praiſed her, she returneth al the praiſe to him.\f* worthie for my beloued to drinke, & for his lippes and his teeth to ruminate.
\v 10 I to my beloued, and his turning is toward me.
\v 11 \f d \fr 7.11 \ft Praying him to come and ſtil remaine vvith her.\f* Come my beloued, let vs goe forth in to the filde, let vs abide in the villages.
\v 12 Let vs riſe early to the vineyards, let vs ſee if the vineyard florishe, if the flowers be readie to bring forth fruites, if the pomegranates florish: there wil I giue thee my breaſts.
\v 13 The Mandragoraes haue geuen a ſmel. In our gates al fruites: \f e \fr 7.13 \ft Acknowleging him to be the only Sauiour of both old and new teſtament.\f* the new and the old, my beloued, I haue kept for thee.
\c 8
\p
\v 1 WHO \f a \fr 8.1 \ft The Synagogue proſecuteth her prayer, deſiring Chriſts Incarnation.\f* shal giue to me thee my brother, ſucking the breaſts of my mother, that I may finde thee without, and kiſſe thee, and now no man deſpiſe me?
\v 2 I wil take hold of thee, and wil bring thee into my mothers houſe: there thou shalt teach me, and I wil giue thee a cuppe of ſpiced wine, and new wine of my pomegranats.
\v 3 His left hand vnder my head, and his right hand shal embrace me.
\v 4 \f b \fr 8.4 \ft Chriſt admonisheth wordlie men not to moleſt thoſe that ſerue him in contemplation, & other ſpiritual vertues.\f* I adiure you ô daughters of Ieruſalem, that you rayſe not vp, nor make the beloued to awake til herſelfe wil.
\v 5 \f c \fr 8.5 \ft Angels and other Sainctes of the triumphant Church admire the beautie of the Gentiles conuerted; which is alſo vnderſtood of euerie holie ſoule aſcending from this world into heauen, And more ſingularly of the moſt glorious virgin mother of God.\f* Who is this, that cometh vp from the deſert, flowing with delightes, leaning vpon her beloued? Vnder the appletree I rayſed thee vp: \f d \fr 8.5 \ft The Synagogue of the Iewes was corrupt vnder the tree of Chriſts Croſſe, when they cried: Crucifie him, Crucifie him. And againe: His bloud be vpon vs, and vpon our children. Againe: VVe haue no King but Cæſar. &c.\f* there thy mother was corrupted, there she was defloured that bare thee.
\v 6 Put me as a ſeale vpon thy hart, as a ſeale vpon thyne arme: becauſe loue is ſtrong as death: ielouſie is hard as hel, the lampes therof lampes of fyre and flames.
\v 7 Manie waters can not quench charitie, neither shal floudes ouerwhelme it: if a man shal giue al the ſubſtance of his houſe for loue, as nothing he shal deſpiſe it.
\v 8 \f e \fr 8.8 \ft Chriſt againe ſheweth his affection towards his Church of the Gentiles: calling her his owne ſiſter, and the Synagogs ſiſter, promiſing and beſtowing on her manie excellent benefites.\f* Our ſiſter is litle, and hath no breaſts. What shal we doe to our ſiſter in the day when she is to be ſpoken vnto?
\v 9 If she be a wal, let vs build vpon it bulwarkes of ſiluer: if she be a doore, let vs ioyne it together with bordes of ceder.
\v 10 \f f \fr 8.10 \ft The Church of Gentiles reioyceth in the ſtrong defence, vvhervvith her Sauiour hath eſtablished her.\f* I am a wal: and my breaſts are aſ a towre, ſince I was made before him as one finding peace.
\v 11 The peacemaker had a vinyard, in that which hath peoples: he deliuered the ſame \f g \fr 8.11 \ft Keepers of this vinyard, vvere the Prophetes and Apoſtles, and their Succeſſors are ſtil the kepers therof.\f* to keepers, a man bringeth for the fruite thereof a thouſand peeces of ſiluer.
\v 12 \f h \fr 8.12 \ft Chriſt sheweth that together vvith the paſtors, himſelf eſpecially hath care of his Church, alvvayes aſſiſting the viſible gouerners therof with his inuiſible grace.\f* My vineyard is before me. A thouſand are thy peacemakers, and two hundreth for them, that keepe the fruites thereof.
\v 13 Thou that dwelleſt in the gardens, the frends doe harken: make me heare thy voice.
\v 14 \f i \fr 8.14 \ft The whole Church militant vvel contented, yea deſiring Chriſts Aſcenſion into heauen, for the good of al that here ſerue him, prayeth him from thence to ſend abundance of his grace, that vve may aſcend the high mountaines of perfect charitie, and zele of Gods honour, that he wil make our ſoules ſuch hilles, the garden of al vertues, & ſo voutſafe to dwel therin. Amen.\f* Flee, ô my beloued, and be like to the roe, and to the fawne of harts vpon the mountaines of aromatical ſpices.
